    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Which Day of Your Madeira Holiday Is Best for a Boat Trip?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>12 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Usually your second or third day. By then you have slept off the flight, and there are still several days left to move the trip to if the sea is too rough. Avoid the days you fly, and never keep the boat for the end of the week as a final treat.</p>

      <h2>Why is the last day the worst one to choose?</h2><p>Because a trip that cannot go has nowhere left to go. When a Chifbay skipper judges the water unsafe, the trip is off, and you get the choice of a later date or all your money back. Early in a holiday that is a minor change of plan. On the final day of your stay it simply means no boat.</p><p>People save the boat for the end because it feels like the highlight. The sea does not know that. A big swell or a strong wind can keep small boats in port for a day or two, and nobody can see it coming a week ahead.</p><p>There is a second, smaller reason. The Insta360 video, the drone footage and the camera photos from a Chifbay trip are much more fun to have in the middle of the holiday, when you can still show them at dinner, than on the way to the airport.</p><h2>Why not the first morning, then?</h2><p>Because the flight in is the least reliable part of any Madeira trip. Wind at the airport, a delay, a missing bag with the swimsuits in it. We wrote a whole guide on <a href="/posts/boat-trip-on-arrival-or-departure-day-madeira">boat trips on arrival or departure day</a>, and the short version is: the day you land is a gamble, and the day you leave only works with an early trip and a late flight.</p><p>Day one is better spent on small things. Walk the seafront, find the marina, which sits in the middle of it, and see how long it takes from your hotel. Then you know the way on the morning of the trip.</p><h2>What does a good week look like with a boat in it?</h2><p>Put the boat early, put a flexible plan right after it, and keep one more spare day later in the week. Here is one way to lay out seven nights in Funchal:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Main plan</th><th>If the boat has to move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Arrive, seafront walk, find the marina</td><td>Nothing booked</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Funchal on foot: old town, market, cable car to Monte</td><td>Easy to swap with day 3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Day Trip at 10:00, slow afternoon</td><td>Moves to day 4</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Drive west or north, Porto Moniz or Pico do Arieiro</td><td>Becomes the boat day</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Levada walk or gardens</td><td>Second spare day</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Free, or the Sunset Trip</td><td>Last safe slot for a moved trip</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fly home</td><td>Too late</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The pattern matters more than the details. The boat sits on day 3, and days 4, 5 and 6 hold plans you would not mind shuffling. On a shorter stay the same idea shrinks: with four nights, aim for day 2 and keep day 3 open.</p><h2>Does the day of the week matter?</h2><p>For the boat, much less than the sea does. For the rest of your plans, a little. A few land plans are tied to weekdays, so it can make sense to put the boat on the day when those are closed.</p><ul><li><strong>Sunday.</strong> The Mercado dos Lavradores, Funchal's farmers' market, is closed, and many small shops shut too. That makes Sunday a good day to be on the water. Our guide to <a href="/posts/things-to-do-in-funchal-on-a-sunday">what you can do in Funchal on a Sunday</a> lists what stays open.</li><li><strong>Saturday.</strong> The market closes in the early afternoon, so keep Saturday morning for it if you want to see it busy.</li><li><strong>Public holidays.</strong> These change opening hours across the island. Check the dates of your stay before you fix the rest of the week.</li></ul><h2>How do you pair the boat with the rest of the day?</h2><p>Give it a soft landing on either side. Two or three hours of sun, wind and two swims leave most people pleasantly tired, not ready for a mountain.</p><h3>With the morning Day Trip</h3><p>Leave at 10:00 and you are ashore again by half past twelve, or by one on the 3h trip. Then lunch in the old town and a quiet afternoon. Do not plan a long drive straight after. Salt, sun and a big lunch make the winding roads feel longer.</p><h3>With the afternoon Day Trip</h3><p>The 14:00 departure suits a slow morning: a late breakfast, a walk through the market, a coffee by the marina. You are back at 16:30 or 17:00 with time for a shower before dinner.</p><h3>With the Sunset Trip</h3><p>Departure follows the sun: 1 h 15 before it sets, which is close to 16:45 in midwinter and close to 20:00 at midsummer. The trip lasts 2h or 2h30. That frees the whole day for something inland. Be back in Funchal with an hour to spare, because mountain roads always take longer than the map says.</p><h3>What not to stack on the same day</h3><p>A sunrise at Pico do Arieiro, which means leaving Funchal in the dark, followed by a morning boat. Or a long levada walk the morning of a Sunset Trip, when the legs are done and the evening on deck turns cool. Either works on paper and feels like too much on the day.</p><h2>When should you book a fixed date anyway?</h2><p>When the date is the point. It depends on why you are going:</p><ul><li><strong>A birthday or an anniversary.</strong> Book the day itself, and keep a fallback in mind. Our guide to a <a href="/posts/birthday-boat-trip-madeira-private-celebration-guide">birthday boat trip in Madeira</a> covers the details.</li><li><strong>A cruise stop.</strong> You have one day in port, so the choice is made for you. Pick the trip that gets you back to the ship with time to spare.</li><li><strong>A very short stay.</strong> With two nights there is no buffer to protect. Book the middle day and accept the risk.</li><li><strong>New Year's Eve.</strong> The fireworks night on 31 December 2026 has its own <a href="/new-years-eve-fireworks-boat-madeira">page for the New Year's Eve boat</a>, and it happens on one date only.</li></ul><p>For everyone else, the rule is simple. Book the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> or the Sunset Trip for the second or third day, then build the rest of the week around it. If the forecast moves, the free cancellation until a day before you sail, plus the spare days you kept, do the rest.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>I only have three days in Madeira. When should I do the boat trip?</h3><p>On day two. It is the only day with no flight in it and still leaves day three as a backup if the sea is too rough.</p><h3>Is a weekday better than a weekend for a boat trip in Madeira?</h3><p>Not for the sea. It can help to put the boat on a Sunday, when Funchal's market and many small shops are closed.</p><h3>Can I move my trip if the forecast changes?</h3><p>Yes. You pay nothing to cancel until 24 hours before you sail, then book another day. If the skipper calls it off because of the sea, a new date or a full refund is your choice.</p><h3>Should the Sunset Trip go early in the week too?</h3><p>Yes, for the same reason. A cloudy or rough evening early on can still move to a clearer one before you fly.</p>

      <blockquote>Day three for the boat, days four to six for the spare.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

